# Copy this file to ".env" and adjust for your setup, then: docker compose up -d
#
# ── Local testing (default) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The web container only exposes port 80 inside Docker. To open it on this machine,
# publish it with a docker-compose.override.yml (see README → Quick start), e.g. 8080:80,
# then open http://localhost:8080
RP_ID=localhost
ORIGIN=http://localhost:8080
RP_NAME=openGym

# ── Real deployment behind your own HTTPS domain ───────────────────────────
# Passkeys are bound to the exact hostname, and browsers only allow them over
# HTTPS (localhost is the one exception). Put openGym behind a reverse proxy /
# tunnel that terminates TLS for your domain, then set:
#
#   RP_ID=gym.example.com
#   ORIGIN=https://gym.example.com
#   RP_NAME=openGym
#
# (Point your proxy / panel — e.g. EasyPanel — at the web service, port 80.
#  See docs/SELF_HOSTING.md.)

# ── Admin dashboard & invite-only signup (optional) ────────────────────────
# Both off by default — a fresh instance has open signup and no admin.
#
# Make yourself an admin: register your own passkey profile first, find your
# user id in ./data/db.json ("users"[].id), then list it here (comma-separated
# for several admins). Admins get an "Admin dashboard" link in Settings to see
# users, disable accounts, generate invite codes and view workout history.
#
#   ADMIN_UIDS=youruserid,anotheradmin
#
# Require an invite code to create new profiles (existing accounts keep working;
# generate the codes from the admin dashboard):
#
#   INVITE_ONLY=1

# ── Session length (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# How long a sign-in lasts, in days. Default 90 — long enough that people who
# train regularly never see a login screen, short enough that a stolen cookie
# doesn't stay good forever. Lower it for an instance on the open internet,
# raise it for a private one. Only affects new sign-ins; cookies already handed
# out keep the lifetime they were issued with. Users can end every session on
# every device themselves with "Sign out everywhere" in Settings.
#
#   SESSION_DAYS=90

# ── Notifications (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Push works out of the box — nothing to set. VAPID keys are generated on first
# run and saved to ./data/vapid.json. Each user's browser reports its own
# timezone automatically, so the workout-day reminder fires at their local
# time regardless of where the server runs or where they're travelling.

# ── AI Coach (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to set. Claude's Agent SDK and the OpenAI Codex CLI are built into the api image;
# neither path needs an API key. Enable one from Settings → Admin → AI Coach.
#
# Claude: on a trusted computer where Claude Code is installed, run `claude setup-token`, then
# paste its value into "Add CLI token". Codex: choose "Sign in with ChatGPT" and complete the
# device-code flow in a trusted browser. Codex keeps its refreshable CLI cache in ./data/codex;
# treat that directory like a password-bearing backup. The app never handles a browser OAuth
# callback. It stays off until an admin turns it on, and each profile has to consent separately.
#
# The one knob here is a kill switch: set this and the Coach reports as disabled
# everywhere regardless of what is stored, which is useful for a fleet of
# instances you do not want the feature on for.
#
#   COACH_DISABLED=1

# ── Levanta: WhatsApp assistant (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# The assistant talks to users over WhatsApp through an Evolution API instance and uses
# Google Gemini to read equipment photos and free-text workout logs. All off until set.
#
# Evolution API (https://doc.evolution-api.com): base URL, instance name and its API key.
#   EVOLUTION_URL=https://evo.example.com
#   EVOLUTION_INSTANCE=levanta
#   EVOLUTION_APIKEY=...
#
# Shared secret for the webhook. In Evolution, set the instance webhook to
#   <ORIGIN>/api/wa/webhook?token=<WA_WEBHOOK_TOKEN>   with the event MESSAGES_UPSERT
# (turn on "Webhook base64" so photos arrive inline). Use a long random string.
#   WA_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=
#
# Gemini (https://aistudio.google.com/apikey). Without it the assistant still works, but it
# skips the equipment photos and only understands "FIZ" (not "supino 3x10 40kg").
#   GEMINI_API_KEY=
#   GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
#
# Timezone for users' reminder times (default America/Sao_Paulo).
#   WA_TZ=America/Sao_Paulo

# ── Database (recommended with WhatsApp) ───────────────────────────────────
# A Postgres container (service "db" in docker-compose.yml) keeps users, WhatsApp profiles, the
# chat log, events and consents. Without DATABASE_URL everything stays in ./data/db.json, which
# is fine for a family instance. On first boot with a database, db.json is migrated into it.
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://levanta:change-me@db:5432/levanta
